1. Physical Sanity Checks

These checks ensure that the mathematical rules of your biological system are not being violated.

Non-negativity (u≥0): You cannot have negative macrophages. Due to the numerical nature of the BDF solver, 
values might occasionally dip slightly below zero (e.g., -1e-12) due to floating-point errors, but any significant negative 
number means the solver took a bad step or the math is fundamentally unstable.

Volume Capacity (0≤ϕ≤1): The extracellular fluid volume fraction ϕ must never drop below 0 (which would mean macrophages are 
physically overlapping/crushed beyond 100% capacity) and should never exceed 1 (which would mean you have more than 100% empty space).

Mass/Cell Conservation (if applicable): If you set the death rates (β=0, γ=0) and ensure no flux at the boundaries, 
the total number of macrophages in your system should remain exactly constant over time, even as they move through space 
and ingest lipid.

2. Numerical Verification Checks

These checks ensure your grid resolution and solver tolerances aren't artificially altering the results.

Grid Independence (Spatial M): If you run the model with M=50 and then with M=100, the overall shape of the curves 
(like total cell density over time) should be nearly identical. If doubling the grid points drastically changes the answer, 
your M is too small to capture the spatial gradients.

Class Resolution Independence (Lipid N): Similarly, changing N=100 to N=200 (while adjusting Δv to keep maximum volume the same) 
should yield the same macroscopic lipid load profiles.

Solver Tolerance Sensitivity: The SciPy solve_ivp uses rtol=1e-6 and atol=1e-9 by default in your code. 
Try running it with rtol=1e-8. If the result changes significantly, your initial tolerances were too loose.
